#{[17592186529958 :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t/3}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t/3},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R"] [17592186529977 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186529983 :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t/3}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t/3},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R"] [17592186529996 :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t/3},\\,{c}_{1}\\in R"] [17592186529998 :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}(t-1) \\\\ (2-t){e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186530013 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ {x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186530038 :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}(2t-1){e}^{5t} \\\\ (t+2){e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186530041 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} \\\\ 3x\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186530059 :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R"] [17592186530061 :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-4t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R"] [17592186530067 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4t{e}^{-3t} \\\\ (1+t){e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186530074 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & x & {x}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186530102 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}t{e}^{-3t} \\\\ (1-2t){e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186530118 :answer/value "\\text{x}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2{e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186530119 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186530121 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}t{e}^{-3t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186530136 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186530155 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4t{e}^{-3t} \\\\ (1-2t){e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186530161 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}t{e}^{-3t} \\\\ (1-t){e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186530169 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6{x}_{1}{x}_{2} & 6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186530180 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{1} & 2{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186530188 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}(1+\\frac{t}{2}){e}^{-2t} \\\\ (2t-1){e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186530189 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2x & 3{x}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186530195 :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R"] [17592186530418 :question/problem "Given the function \n$f(x;w)={w}_{1}{x}_{1}+{w}_{2}{x}_{2},$\ncompute ${∇}_{w}\\,f$ with the input $$\n\\displaystyle x={\\begin{bmatrix}5,\\,3\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}.\n$$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">${∇}_{w}\\,f=\\text{ }$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186530418 :question/worked-solution "To compute ${∇}_{w}\\,f$, we take partial derivatives with respect to each component of $w.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{1}}({w}_{1}{x}_{1}+{w}_{2}{x}_{2})={x}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{2}}({w}_{1}{x}_{1}+{w}_{2}{x}_{2})={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, with $$\n\\displaystyle x={\\begin{bmatrix}5,\\,3\\end{bmatrix}}^{T},\n$$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{w}\\,f & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186530419 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\,{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}x(t).\n$$"] [17592186530419 :question/worked-solution "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the matrix of our differential equation is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t/3}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t/3},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$"] [17592186530420 :question/problem "Consider a function $f:{R}^{3\\times 3}\\to {R}^{3}$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(A)=A\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nGiven that ${a}_{ij}$ denotes the entry of $A$ in the intersection of the $i$th row and $j$th column, we have\n\n$\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{a}_{31}}=${{field-1}}."] [17592186530420 :question/worked-solution "We can write the function $f$ as\n\n$$\nf(A)=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} & {a}_{13} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22} & {a}_{23} \\\\ {a}_{31} & {a}_{32} & {a}_{33}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5{a}_{11}+{a}_{12}+4{a}_{13} \\\\ 5{a}_{21}+{a}_{22}+4{a}_{23} \\\\ 5{a}_{31}+{a}_{32}+4{a}_{33}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{a}_{31}}=\\frac{∂}{∂{a}_{31}}(5{a}_{31}+{a}_{32}+4{a}_{33})=5.\n$$"] [17592186530421 :question/problem "Consider the function $f:{R}^{3}\\to R$ defined by $f(x)={x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+{x}_{2}{x}_{3}.$ What is the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ at $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [17592186530421 :question/worked-solution "The total derivative of a scalar-valued function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ with respect to a vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is the row vector\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}\\in {R}^{1\\times n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the function \n\n- acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{3},$ and\n\n- outputs a scalar $f(x)\\in R$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)={x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+{x}_{2}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{3}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+{x}_{2}{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+{x}_{2}{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{3}}({x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{1}{x}_{3}+{x}_{2}{x}_{3})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} & {x}_{1}+{x}_{3} & 3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\in {R}^{1\\times 3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the total derivative at $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}({x}_{0}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}0+3(2) & 1+2 & 3(1)+0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186530425 :question/worked-solution "To compute ${∇}_{a,b}\\,f$, we take partial derivatives with respect to $a$ and $b.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂a} & =\\frac{∂}{∂a}(a{x}^{2}+bx)={x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂b} & =\\frac{∂}{∂b}(a{x}^{2}+bx)=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{a,b}\\,f & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂a} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂b}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2} \\\\ x\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186530427 :question/problem "Consider the function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot x,\n$$\n\nwhere the dot denotes matrix multiplication. What is the shape of the total derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}?$"] [17592186530427 :question/worked-solution "The total derivative of a vector-valued function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ with respect to a vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}\\in {R}^{m\\times n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the function multiplies a vector by a $3\\times 3$ matrix. Thus, \n\n- it must act on $3$-dimensional column vectors as input, and\n\n- output a $3$-dimensional column vector.\n\nTherefore, $f:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3},$ and the total derivative will be a $3\\times 3$ matrix."] [17592186530430 :question/problem "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot x,\n$$\n\nwhere the dot denotes matrix multiplication. What is the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $x?$"] [17592186530430 :question/worked-solution "The total derivative of a scalar-valued function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ with respect to a vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ is the row vector\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}\\in {R}^{1\\times n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, writing out the function $f(x)$ where $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} & {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}+3{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} & 4{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\in {R}^{1\\times 2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186530434 :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}{e}^{-5t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{-5t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on that line and approach the origin as as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=x$ is $\\text{a stable}$ line.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=2x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on that line and approach the origin as as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=2x$ is $\\text{a stable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a stable node (sink)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/33/330e2a1dd9f7d2cbcadb61fe49e767d95ee76a2cb669a9917ef3f6333719b98f.png)"] [17592186530436 :question/problem "![](images/51/51ffadb1da41e6a1fe06fdc9c14a6967d3a80b4d26c6a830a401105e0f18bbf5.png)\n\nThe phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two real, distinct eigenvalues is shown above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[-1,2]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[1,2]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ has the equation\n\n$$\ny={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing \n{{field-2}},\nmeaning that the solutions of the form \n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nmust approach \n{{field-3}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have \n{{field-4}}.\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ has the equation\n\n$$\ny={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing \n{{field-6}},\nmeaning that the solutions of the form \n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nmust approach \n{{field-7}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have \n{{field-8}}.\n\nFinally, since the solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector {{field-9}} as they move towards the origin, the eigenvalue that determines the long-term behavior (dominant eigenvalue) is \n{{field-10}}.\n\nTherefore,\n{{field-11}} \n$<0.$"] [17592186530436 :question/worked-solution "The straight-line solutions of the system have the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ has the equation\n\n$$\ny=-2x,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/6e/6ea2d64a390187a28cfcd1c2692e97e5dc2ca5cb37649ba12cf71864e36958ea.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing $\\text{toward the origin},$ meaning that the solutions of the form \n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nmust approach $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}<0.$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ has the equation\n\n$$\ny=2x,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/2f/2f2340cbff80765a5d3b551e87eb4207eebad05970e366ceeccd7bb142c0b677.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing $\\text{toward the origin},$ meaning that the solutions of the form \n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nmust approach $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}<0.$\n\nSo, for this particular system, both eigenvalues are negative. The eigenvalue that determines the long-term behavior (dominant eigenvalue) is the one closest to $0.$\n\nNow, since the solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector ${v}_{1}$ as they move towards the origin, the eigenvalue that determines the long-term behavior is ${\\lambda }_{1}.$\n\nTherefore, ${\\lambda }_{2}<{\\lambda }_{1}<0.$"] [17592186530441 :question/problem "Consider a function $f:{R}^{3\\times 3}\\to {R}^{3}$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(A)=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -6 & -5\\end{bmatrix}A.\n$$\n\nGiven that ${a}_{ij}$ denotes the entry of $A$ in the intersection of the $i$th row and $j$th column, we have\n\n$\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{a}_{33}}=${{field-1}}."] [17592186530441 :question/worked-solution "We can write the function $f$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(A) & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -6 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} & {a}_{13} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22} & {a}_{23} \\\\ {a}_{31} & {a}_{32} & {a}_{33}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10{a}_{11}-6{a}_{21}-5{a}_{31}\\, & 10{a}_{12}-6{a}_{22}-5{a}_{32}\\, & 10{a}_{13}-6{a}_{23}-5{a}_{33}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂{a}_{33}}=\\frac{∂}{∂{a}_{33}}(10{a}_{13}-6{a}_{23}-5{a}_{33})=-5.\n$$"] [17592186530443 :question/worked-solution "To compute ${∇}_{x,y}\\,f$, we take partial derivatives with respect to $x$ and $y$ only.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{xyz}) & =yz{e}^{xyz} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{xyz}) & =xz{e}^{xyz}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x,y}\\,f & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}yz{e}^{xyz} \\\\ xz{e}^{xyz}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186530447 :question/problem "Consider the function $f,$ defined as \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}x-1 \\\\ {x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the shape of the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $x?$"] [17592186530447 :question/worked-solution "The total derivative of a vector-valued function $f:R\\to {R}^{m}$ with respect to a scalar $x$ is the column vector\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂x}\\end{bmatrix}\\in {R}^{m\\times 1}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the function \n\n- acts on a scalar $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- outputs a vector $f(x)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the total derivative will be a column vector of shape $3\\times 1.$"] [17592186530452 :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186530452 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-2$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-2+2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2+2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}-1{w}_{2}=1 \\\\ 0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{2}=-1.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{1}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{1}=0,$  we get\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-2t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $$\n\\displaystyle x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ -{c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ -{c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(1+t){e}^{-2t} \\\\ -{e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186530460 :question/worked-solution "First, we compute ${∇}_{w}\\,f$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{1}}({w}_{1}-{w}_{2}{x}_{1}{x}_{2})^{2}=2({w}_{1}-{w}_{2}{x}_{1}{x}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{2}}({w}_{1}-{w}_{2}{x}_{1}{x}_{2})^{2}=-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}({w}_{1}-{w}_{2}{x}_{1}{x}_{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the second derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{1}^{2}}=2\\,\\, &  & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{1}∂{w}_{2}}=-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\  & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{2}∂{w}_{1}}=-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}\\,\\, &  & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{2}^{2}}=2{x}_{1}^{2}{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{w}({∇}_{w}\\,f) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{1}∂{w}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{2}∂{w}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{2}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2{x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\ -2{x}_{1}{x}_{2} & 2{x}_{1}^{2}{x}_{2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186530461 :question/worked-solution "To compute ${∇}_{a,b}\\,f$, we take partial derivatives with respect to $a$ and $b.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂a} & =\\frac{∂}{∂a}(axy+b)=xy \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂b} & =\\frac{∂}{∂b}(axy+b)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{a,b}\\,f & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂a} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂b}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}xy \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186530463 :question/worked-solution "To compute ${∇}_{x,z}\\,f$, we take partial derivatives with respect to $x$ and $z$ only.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(zw{e}^{xy}) & =yzw{e}^{xy} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}=\\frac{∂}{∂z}(zw{e}^{xy}) & =w{e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x,z}\\,f & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}yzw{e}^{xy} \\\\ w{e}^{xy}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186530466 :question/worked-solution "To compute ${∇}_{a,b}\\,f$, we take partial derivatives with respect to $a$ and $b.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂a} & =\\frac{∂}{∂a}(a{x}^{2}+bx+c)={x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂b} & =\\frac{∂}{∂b}(a{x}^{2}+bx+c)=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{a,b}\\,f & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂a} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂b}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2} \\\\ x\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186530467 :question/worked-solution "To compute ${∇}_{x,z}\\,f$, we take partial derivatives with respect to $x$ and $z$ only.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{3}+{y}^{3}z) & ={y}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}=\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{y}^{3}+{y}^{3}z) & ={y}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x,z}\\,f & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{y}^{3} \\\\ {y}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186530468 :question/problem "Given the function \n$f(x;w)={w}_{1}{x}_{1}^{2}+{w}_{2}{x}_{2}^{2}+{w}_{3}{x}_{3}^{2},$\ncompute ${∇}_{w}\\,f$ with the input $$\n\\displaystyle x={\\begin{bmatrix}-1,\\,-2,\\,-3\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}.\n$$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">${∇}_{w}\\,f=\\text{ }$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186530468 :question/worked-solution "To compute ${∇}_{w}\\,f$, we take partial derivatives with respect to each component of $w.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{1}}({w}_{1}{x}_{1}^{2}+{w}_{2}{x}_{2}^{2}+{w}_{3}{x}_{3}^{2})={x}_{1}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{2}}({w}_{1}{x}_{1}^{2}+{w}_{2}{x}_{2}^{2}+{w}_{3}{x}_{3}^{2})={x}_{2}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{3}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{3}}({w}_{1}{x}_{1}^{2}+{w}_{2}{x}_{2}^{2}+{w}_{3}{x}_{3}^{2})={x}_{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, with $$\n\\displaystyle x={\\begin{bmatrix}-1,\\,-2,\\,-3\\end{bmatrix}}^{T},\n$$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{w}\\,f & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}^{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}^{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186530472 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\,{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}x(t).\n$$"] [17592186530472 :question/worked-solution "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the matrix of our differential equation is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-4t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-4t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$"] [17592186530474 :question/worked-solution "To compute ${∇}_{y,z}\\,f$, we take partial derivatives with respect to $y$ and $z$ only.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{xy}{z}\\right) & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}=\\frac{∂}{∂z}\\left(\\frac{xy}{z}\\right) & =-\\frac{xy}{{z}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{y,z}\\,f & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ -\\frac{xy}{{z}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186530479 :question/problem "Consider the function $f:R\\to {R}^{2},$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}?$"] [17592186530479 :question/worked-solution "The total derivative of a vector-valued function $f:R\\to {R}^{m}$ with respect to a scalar $x$ is the column vector\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂x}\\end{bmatrix}\\in {R}^{m\\times 1}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the function \n\n- acts on a scalar $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- outputs a vector $f(x)\\in {R}^{2}$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\end{bmatrix}\\in {R}^{2\\times 1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the total derivative at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186530486 :question/problem "Consider the function $f,$ defined as \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere the dot denotes matrix multiplication.\nWhat is the shape of the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $x?$"] [17592186530486 :question/worked-solution "The total derivative of a vector-valued function $f:R\\to {R}^{m}$ with respect to a scalar $x$ is the column vector\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂x}\\end{bmatrix}\\in {R}^{m\\times 1}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the function \n\n- acts on a scalar $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- outputs a vector $f(x)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the total derivative will be a column vector of shape $3\\times 1.$"] [17592186530490 :question/problem "Given the function \n$f(x;w)={w}_{1}{x}_{1}+{w}_{2}{x}_{2}-{w}_{3}{x}_{3},$\ncompute ${∇}_{w}\\,f$ with the input $$\n\\displaystyle x={\\begin{bmatrix}3,\\,2,\\,1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}.\n$$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">${∇}_{w}\\,f=\\text{ }$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186530490 :question/worked-solution "To compute ${∇}_{w}\\,f$, we take partial derivatives with respect to each component of $w.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{1}}({w}_{1}{x}_{1}+{w}_{2}{x}_{2}-{w}_{3}{x}_{3})={x}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{2}}({w}_{1}{x}_{1}+{w}_{2}{x}_{2}-{w}_{3}{x}_{3})={x}_{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{3}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{3}}({w}_{1}{x}_{1}+{w}_{2}{x}_{2}-{w}_{3}{x}_{3})=-{x}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, with $$\n\\displaystyle x={\\begin{bmatrix}3,\\,2,\\,1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T},\n$$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{w}\\,f & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186530496 :question/worked-solution "To compute ${∇}_{a,c}\\,f$, we take partial derivatives with respect to $a$ and $c.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂a} & =\\frac{∂}{∂a}(a{x}^{3}+b{x}^{2}+cx)={x}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂c} & =\\frac{∂}{∂c}(a{x}^{3}+b{x}^{2}+cx)=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{a,c}\\,f & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂a} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂c}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{3} \\\\ x\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186530498 :question/problem "Consider a function $f:{R}^{3\\times 2}\\to {R}^{2}$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(A)=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}A.\n$$\n\nGiven that ${a}_{ij}$ denotes the entry of $A$ in the intersection of the $i$th row and $j$th column, we have\n\n$\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{a}_{32}}=${{field-1}}."] [17592186530498 :question/worked-solution "We can write the function $f$ as\n\n$$\nf(A)=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22} \\\\ {a}_{31} & {a}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5{a}_{11}+{a}_{21}-2{a}_{31} & 5{a}_{12}+{a}_{22}-2{a}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{a}_{32}}=\\frac{∂}{∂{a}_{32}}(5{a}_{11}+{a}_{21}-2{a}_{31})=0.\n$$"] [17592186530500 :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186530500 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =5$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle v=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}5-5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 5-5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=0 \\\\ -1{w}_{1}+0{w}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=-1.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{5t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $$\n\\displaystyle x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{5t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{5t}. \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{5t} \\\\ (2+t){e}^{5t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186530511 :question/worked-solution "First, we compute ${∇}_{w}\\,f$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{1}}({w}_{1}{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-{w}_{2})^{2}=2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}({w}_{1}{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-{w}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{2}}({w}_{1}{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-{w}_{2})^{2}=-2({w}_{1}{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-{w}_{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the second derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{1}^{2}}=2{x}_{1}^{2}{x}_{2}^{2}\\,\\, &  & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{1}∂{w}_{2}}=-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\  & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{2}∂{w}_{1}}=-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}\\,\\, &  & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{2}^{2}}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{w}({∇}_{w}\\,f) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{1}∂{w}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{2}∂{w}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{w}_{2}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{1}^{2}{x}_{2}^{2} & -2{x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\ -2{x}_{1}{x}_{2} & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186530513 :question/problem "Consider the function $f:R\\to {R}^{3},$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ {x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $x?$"] [17592186530513 :question/worked-solution "The total derivative of a vector-valued function $f:R\\to {R}^{m}$ with respect to a scalar $x$ is the column vector\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂x}\\end{bmatrix}\\in {R}^{m\\times 1}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the function \n\n- acts on a scalar $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- outputs a vector $f(x)\\in {R}^{3}$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ {x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂x}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2x \\\\ 3{x}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\in {R}^{3\\times 1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186530517 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}x(t).\n$$\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ a\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ a\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{4t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$"] [17592186530517 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda =4$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}4-4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 4-4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5{w}_{2}=1 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{2}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{1}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{1}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{4t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=0,b=\\frac{1}{5},$ and $a+b=\\frac{1}{5}.$"] [17592186530518 :question/worked-solution "To compute ${∇}_{a,b}\\,f$, we take partial derivatives with respect to $a$ and $b.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂a} & =\\frac{∂}{∂a}(a{x}^{2}+bxy)={x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂b} & =\\frac{∂}{∂b}(a{x}^{2}+bxy)=xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{a,b}\\,f & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂a} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂b}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2} \\\\ xy\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186530525 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\,{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}x(t).\n$$"] [17592186530525 :question/worked-solution "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the matrix of our differential equation is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$"] [17592186530527 :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=\\frac{1}{2}x$ is $\\text{an unstable}$ line.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=-x$ is $\\text{an unstable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{an unstable node (source)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/e5/e5c236defa66a00f12cd338ed0012cf91e4d7c15aa4865fc2b1edf5b0e2ea1da.png)"] [17592186530529 :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of the system's matrix is $\\lambda =-3.$*"] [17592186530529 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-3$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-2+3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -4+3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}+{w}_{2}=-1 \\\\ -{w}_{1}-{w}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=-1-{w}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get ${w}_{1}=-1,$ and therefore\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $$\n\\displaystyle x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={e}^{-3t}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-3t}-\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}t{e}^{-3t} \\\\ (1-t){e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186530530 :question/worked-solution "First, we take the variable subset gradient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x,z}\\,f & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2x \\\\ 2z\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the second derivatives with respect to $x$ and $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x)=2\\,\\, &  & \\frac{∂}{∂z}(2x)=0 \\\\  & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2z)=0\\,\\, &  & \\frac{∂}{∂z}(2z)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x,z}({∇}_{x,z}\\,f) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂z} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂z∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{z}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186530532 :question/problem "Consider a function $f:{R}^{3\\times 2}\\to {R}^{2}$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(A)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}A.\n$$\n\nGiven that ${a}_{ij}$ denotes the entry of $A$ in the intersection of the $i$th row and $j$th column, we have\n\n$\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{a}_{22}}=${{field-1}}."] [17592186530532 :question/worked-solution "We can write the function $f$ as\n\n$$\nf(A)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22} \\\\ {a}_{31} & {a}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2{a}_{11}-2{a}_{21}+3{a}_{31} & 2{a}_{12}-2{a}_{22}+3{a}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{a}_{22}}=\\frac{∂}{∂{a}_{22}}(2{a}_{12}-2{a}_{22}+3{a}_{32})=-2.\n$$"] [17592186530536 :question/problem "Given the function \n$f(x;w)={w}_{1}{x}_{1}-{w}_{2}{x}_{2}+{w}_{3}{x}_{3},$\ncompute ${∇}_{w}\\,f$ with the input $$\n\\displaystyle x={\\begin{bmatrix}-1,\\,-2,\\,4\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}.\n$$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">${∇}_{w}\\,f=\\text{ }$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186530536 :question/worked-solution "To compute ${∇}_{w}\\,f$, we take partial derivatives with respect to each component of $w.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{1}}({w}_{1}{x}_{1}-{w}_{2}{x}_{2}+{w}_{3}{x}_{3})={x}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{2}}({w}_{1}{x}_{1}-{w}_{2}{x}_{2}+{w}_{3}{x}_{3})=-{x}_{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂{w}_{3}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{w}_{3}}({w}_{1}{x}_{1}-{w}_{2}{x}_{2}+{w}_{3}{x}_{3})={x}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, with $$\n\\displaystyle x={\\begin{bmatrix}-1,\\,-2,\\,4\\end{bmatrix}}^{T},\n$$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{w}\\,f & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ -{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186530538 :question/worked-solution "First, we take the variable subset gradient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x,y}\\,f & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{yz}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{yz})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{yz} \\\\ xz{e}^{yz}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the second derivatives with respect to $x$ and $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{yz})=0\\,\\, &  & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{yz})=z{e}^{yz} \\\\  & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(xz{e}^{yz})=z{e}^{yz}\\,\\, &  & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(xz{e}^{yz})=x{z}^{2}{e}^{yz}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x,y}({∇}_{x,y}\\,f) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & z{e}^{yz} \\\\ z{e}^{yz} & x{z}^{2}{e}^{yz}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186530543 :question/problem "Consider the function $f,$ defined as \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the shape of the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $x?$"] [17592186530543 :question/worked-solution "The total derivative of a vector-valued function $f:R\\to {R}^{m}$ with respect to a scalar $x$ is the column vector\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂x}\\end{bmatrix}\\in {R}^{m\\times 1}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the function \n\n- acts on a scalar $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- outputs a vector $f(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the total derivative will be a column vector of shape $2\\times 1.$"] [17592186530786 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(-9i+j-10k)-(-4i-3j-4k) \\\\  & =-5i+4j-6k \\\\  & =⟨-5,4,-6⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(-5i-5j-11k)-(-4i-3j-4k) \\\\  & =-i-2j-7k \\\\  & =⟨-1,-2,-7⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -5 & 4 & -6 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -6 \\\\ -2 & -7\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & -6 \\\\ -1 & -7\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(4\\cdot (-7)-(-6)\\cdot (-2))i-((-5)\\cdot (-7)-(-6)\\cdot (-1))j+((-5)\\cdot (-2)-4\\cdot (-1))k \\\\  & =-40i-29j+14k \\\\  & =⟨-40,-29,14⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-40)^{2}+(-29)^{2}+{14}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2637} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{293}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{3\\sqrt{293}}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186530789 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(2j-2k)-(-i+4j-2k) \\\\  & =i-2j \\\\  & =⟨1,-2,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(-i+2j-3k)-(-i+4j-2k) \\\\  & =-2j-k \\\\  & =⟨0,-2,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot (-2))i-(1\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot 0)j+(1\\cdot (-2)-(-2)\\cdot 0)k \\\\  & =2i+j-2k \\\\  & =⟨2,1,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186530792 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(5i-7j-3k)-(4i-2j+3k) \\\\  & =i-5j-6k \\\\  & =⟨1,-5,-6⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(8i-5j+9k)-(4i-2j+3k) \\\\  & =4i-3j+6k \\\\  & =⟨4,-3,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -5 & -6 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & -6 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -6 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -5 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-5)\\cdot 6-(-6)\\cdot (-3))i-(1\\cdot 6-(-6)\\cdot 4)j+(1\\cdot (-3)-(-5)\\cdot 4)k \\\\  & =-48i-30j+17k \\\\  & =⟨-48,-30,17⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-48)^{2}+(-30)^{2}+{17}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3493}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{3493}}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186530811 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(-i-5j+k)-(-4j+k) \\\\  & =-i-j \\\\  & =⟨-1,-1,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(-2j+3k)-(-4j+k) \\\\  & =2j+2k \\\\  & =⟨0,2,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 2)i-((-1)\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 0)j+((-1)\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 0)k \\\\  & =-2i+2j-2k \\\\  & =⟨-2,2,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{2}=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$"] [17592186530812 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(10i)-(3i-2j+4k) \\\\  & =7i+2j-4k \\\\  & =⟨7,2,-4⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(6i-7j+6k)-(3i-2j+4k) \\\\  & =3i-5j+2k \\\\  & =⟨3,-5,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 7 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}7 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -5\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 2-(-4)\\cdot (-5))i-(7\\cdot 2-(-4)\\cdot 3)j+(7\\cdot (-5)-2\\cdot 3)k \\\\  & =-16i-26j-41k \\\\  & =⟨-16,-26,-41⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-16)^{2}+(-26)^{2}+(-41)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2613}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{2613}}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186530819 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(-i-2j-k)-(-2i-2j-3k) \\\\  & =i+2k \\\\  & =⟨1,0,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(-2i-4j-2k)-(-2i-2j-3k) \\\\  & =-2j+k \\\\  & =⟨0,-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-2))i-(1\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 0)j+(1\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot 0)k \\\\  & =4i-j-2k \\\\  & =⟨4,-1,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186530826 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(3i-j-2k)-(2i+k) \\\\  & =i-j-3k \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,-3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(-i-2j-6k)-(2i+k) \\\\  & =-3i-2j-7k \\\\  & =⟨-3,-2,-7⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & -7\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -7\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot (-7)-(-3)\\cdot (-2))i-(1\\cdot (-7)-(-3)\\cdot (-3))j+(1\\cdot (-2)-(-1)\\cdot (-3))k \\\\  & =i+16j-5k \\\\  & =⟨1,16,-5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{16}^{2}+(-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{282}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{282}}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186530839 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(3i+2j-2k)-(i+2j-2k) \\\\  & =2i \\\\  & =⟨2,0,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(2i+2j)-(i+2j-2k) \\\\  & =i+2k \\\\  & =⟨1,0,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 0)i-(2\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 1)j+(2\\cdot 0-0\\cdot 1)k \\\\  & =-4j \\\\  & =⟨0,-4,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{4}{2}=2.\n$$"] [17592186530844 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(-2k)-(2i+j-2k) \\\\  & =-2i-j \\\\  & =⟨-2,-1,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(2i+3j)-(2i+j-2k) \\\\  & =2j+2k \\\\  & =⟨0,2,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 2)i-((-2)\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 0)j+((-2)\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 0)k \\\\  & =-2i+4j-4k \\\\  & =⟨-2,4,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{4}^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{6}{2}=3.\n$$"] [17592186530848 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(-6i+j-6k)-(-3i-3j-4k) \\\\  & =-3i+4j-2k \\\\  & =⟨-3,4,-2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(-5i-2j-10k)-(-3i-3j-4k) \\\\  & =-2i+j-6k \\\\  & =⟨-2,1,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -6\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -6\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =(4\\cdot (-6)-(-2)\\cdot 1)i-((-3)\\cdot (-6)-(-2)\\cdot (-2))j+((-3)\\cdot 1-4\\cdot (-2))k \\\\  & =-22i-14j+5k \\\\  & =⟨-22,-14,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-22)^{2}+(-14)^{2}+{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{705}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{705}}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186530879 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}28 & 63 \\\\ -82 & -100\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186530903 :answer/value "{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186530908 :answer/value "{e}^{t}(t\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix})"]}
